# Tridion extension conventions

## Module structure

- Export `ExtensionModule` from `src/index.ts` with `initializeGlobals` + `initialize(builder)`
- Register features in separate modules that receive `ExtensionBuilder`
- Use `createExtensionGlobals()` pattern from examples

## UI components

- Match the UI patterns and dependencies from the scaffolded base example (typically `styled-components`)
- Do not use `@tridion/graphene` in generated extensions — it is not publicly available

## Manifest and build paths

- Manifest `main` and `files` use Windows backslashes: `dist\\{extension-name}\\main.js`
- Webpack output path must match `dist/{extension-name}`

## Dependencies

- Match `peerDependencies` and `@tridion-sites/extensions-cli` versions from `.cursor/skills/build-tridion-sites-extension/versioning-notes.md`
- Do not invent package versions — copy pins from the matching `sites-*` example

## Configuration-first

- Externalize deployer-tunable values to `{addonId}.config.json`
- Read via `getConfiguration<T>()` from `globals.ts`
- Export typed config interface alongside registration code

## Priority in config

When `useFormField`, `useContentEditorView`, or equivalent hooks support `priority`:

```typescript
priority: config?.priority,  // never hardcode priority: 1
```

Include default in config file; document when omitted.

## Translations

- Add via `builder.translations.addTranslation`

## What stays in code

Extension contribution IDs, registration structure, React components, translation keys, and business logic — not environment-specific values.
